Interesting — the UEFI smoke boot now FAILS with:

```
APIC initialization failed; IRQs remain disabled: InvalidIoApicVersion
```

This is my mask_all_io_apic_entries change! The mask-all-120 approach reads redirection registers for pins beyond the actual I/O APIC's redirection count. On this I/O APIC (version has max_redirection_index < 119?), reading registers beyond its MRE returns garbage or faults... no — the failure is `InvalidIoApicVersion` returned by mask_all_io_apic_entries via `redirection_registers(pin, IO_APIC_MAX_REDIRECTIONS - 1)` — wait, that only fails if pin > max or pin >= 120 — pin goes 0..119 and max = 119 → redirection_registers(pin, 119): `pin > max_redirection_index` never true (pin ≤ 119)... and `pin >= IO_APIC_MAX_REDIRECTIONS` (120) never true. So redirection_registers can't fail for pin 0..119. Then InvalidIoApicVersion must come from... wait — redirection_registers does `IO_APIC_REDIR_BASE.checked_add(pin.checked_mul(2)?)?` and `high > IO_APIC_REDIR_MAX` check? Let me re-read redirection_registers fully. Earlier I saw at 530: `if pin > max_redirection_index || pin >= IO_APIC_MAX_REDIRECTIONS` and 535: `if high > IO_APIC_REDIR_MAX`. For pin=119: low = 0x10 + 119*2 = 0xFE; high = 0xFF → `high > IO_APIC_REDIR_MAX` where IO_APIC_REDIR_MAX = 0xfe → 0xff > 0xfe → None → Err → InvalidIoApicVersion! So pin 119's high register (0xff) exceeds IO_APIC_REDIR_MAX = 0xfe → fails.

So the architectural register space: redirection entry i uses registers 0x10+2i (low) and 0x10+2i+1 (high); max redirection count architecturally is 240 entries → register up to 0x10+2*239+1 = 0x1FF. But Intel architecture says I/O APIC supports up to 240 redirection entries; max possible MRE = 239 (MRE field is 8 bits, but architecturally capped? The spec says up to 240). This codebase caps at 120 (IO_APIC_MAX_REDIRECTIONS=120) and IO_APIC_REDIR_MAX=0xfe — the highest register address allowed is 0x10 + 2*119 + 1 = 0xff, allowed to exceed? They set REDIR_MAX = 0xfe... 0x10+2*119 = 0xfe (low reg of pin 119) and high = 0xff. Their redirection_registers disallows high = 0xff — that's their own bound. So pin 119 can't be addressed by design (they allow pins 0..118 with high ≤ 0xfe? for pin=118: low=0xfc, high=0xfd ✓; pin 119: high=0xff ✗). So max usable pin under their bound = 118?? But they allowed max_redirection_index < 120 check and pins 0..max where max<120... for max_redirection_index = 118: fine. For 119: pins 0..=119, redirection_registers(119, 119) → pin 119 > 119? No (119 > 119 false); pin >= 120? no; then high = 0xff > 0xfe → None → error. So a MADT/hw reporting MRE=119 would ALSO fail today with the version check — their bound effectively caps at 118. Whatever — the real issue: my mask-all loop hits pin 119 and errors out → APIC init fails → IRQs disabled → smoke test fails.

Fix: in mask_all_io_apic_entries, only mask registers while they exist: pin in 0..=min(max_redirection) hmm. The pragmatic approach: mask entries 0..=119 where addressable (skip the unaddressable pin 119 high reg issue — actually for pin 119: low reg = 0xfe ≤ 0xfe OK; only the high reg is 0xff > 0xfe. Do we NEED to mask the high register? The mask bit is in the LOW dword. Writing low dword mask suffices to mask an entry! The original code read low and wrote `current | IO_APIC_REDIR_MASKED` — no high-reg write. My redirection_registers call requires both to be valid... let me re-read redirection_registers returning (low, high) Option — in mask loop I only use `low`. I can avoid the `high > REDIR_MAX` failure by not using redirection_registers at all for the low register: compute low = IO_APIC_REDIR_BASE + pin*2 with checked ops inline:

```rust
unsafe fn mask_all_io_apic_entries(base: u64) -> Result<(), ApicError> {
    // Mask every architecturally possible redirection entry, not just the
    // hardware-reported count. Only the low register carries the mask bit; it
    // is addressable for all 120 possible entries (0x10 + 2 * 119 = 0xfe).
    let mut pin = 0u8;
    while pin < IO_APIC_MAX_REDIRECTIONS {
        let low = IO_APIC_REDIR_BASE
            .checked_add(pin.checked_mul(2).ok_or(ApicError::InvalidIoApicVersion)?)
            .ok_or(ApicError::InvalidIoApicVersion)?;
        if low > IO_APIC_REDIR_MAX {
            return Err(ApicError::InvalidIoApicVersion);
        }
        let current = unsafe { io_apic_read(base, low) };
        unsafe { io_apic_write(base, low, current | IO_APIC_REDIR_MASKED) };
        pin += 1;
    }
    Ok(())
}
```
For pin=119: low = 0x10 + 238 = 0xfe ≤ 0xfe ✓. This masks every entry without touching high registers ✓. Also removes reliance on redirection_registers for this path.

Hmm, but also the deeper question: reading register 0xfe on an I/O APIC whose MRE is small (e.g., 0 = 1 entry, typical ISA): register 0xfe is architecturally out of range for that device — what does hardware return? For real chipsets, out-of-MRE redirection registers are... unspecified. In QEMU's implementation of the I/O APIC (qemu-system-x86 hw/intc/ioapic.c): ioapic_read for reg ≥ IOAPIC_REDIRECT_COUNT*2+... QEMU has `IOAPIC_REDIRECT_ENTRY_COUNT = 24`? Let me recall QEMU: `#define IOAPIC_VERSION 0x11`, `IOAPIC_REDIRECT_ENTRY_COUNT 24`? Hmm — qemu ioapic implements 24 redirection registers? Older q35/i440fx I/O APIC has 24 redirects. Register reads beyond: QEMU returns 0x0? Or errors? If QEMU returns garbage or traps... The read happens via MMIO — reading an unimplemented register in QEMU typically returns 0 without faulting. Writing mask to nonexistent entries — QEMU's ioapic_write with reg out of range: it might warn/ignore. The UEFI smoke failure earlier was at the mask loop itself returning Err due to the 0xff check (before any MMIO happened — redirection_registers failed first). After my fix, we'd do MMIO writes to registers 0x10..0xfe — on QEMU this happens... let's just try it. If QEMU mishandles it (unexpected APIC bus errors — x86 I/O APIC MMIO never faults; UC reads return garbage/0), it should be benign. The mask write of garbage|0x10000 is fine even if the read returned 0.

But hold on — is writing to entries beyond MRE safe for a REAL I/O APIC? The mask bit write on a non-existent entry — architecturally the register doesn't exist; behavior is ignored/undefined but cannot corrupt the device (it's a per-entry register file). The concern is only: does the I/O APIC allow 8-bit high reg addresses 0x10..0xfe — yes, register select is 8-bit. Harmless in practice; the alternative (only masking 0..=min(MRE, 119)) leaves the "small MRE on hostile firmware" hole... well, no hole — those entries don't exist either. Honestly both work; mask-all is what the audit recommended and what I documented. Keep it, fix the low-only computation.

Actually wait — let me reconsider once more whether MMIO writes beyond MRE could wedge a real Intel I/O APIC. Intel SDM: "The number of redirection entries is indicated by the MRE field... registers beyond this are reserved." Writing reserved registers: undefined, may be ignored. Reserved register reads on MMIO return undefined values, no fault. It won't corrupt other registers (each register is individually addressable by IOREGSEL). The risk accepted by the audit recommendation and my implementation. OK.

Also note — SAME issue applies to `redirection_registers` usage elsewhere? unmask_isa_irq / configure use pin ≤ max_index which comes from version check (max < 120 and registers valid for actual hardware) ✓ unchanged.

Fix mask_all_io_apic_entries now.

